<h1 class="ct-art-title">Where faith meets identity: the Kingdom Life rebrand</h1>
<p class="ct-art-dek">One crown, four city identities and five ministry sub-brands for a growing church.</p>
<div class="ct-art-meta"><img alt="" src="/img/team-subomi.webp"/><span>Olasubomi Archibong</span><span class="dot"></span><span>5 min read</span></div>
</div>
<img alt="Kingdom Life Church brand identity system: one crown, a whole family of brands" class="ct-art-cover" src="/img/work-kingdom-life-v3.webp"/>
</div>
</section>
<section class="section ct-art-body">
<div class="container w-layout-blockcontainer">
<div class="ct-art-col">
<div class="ct-art-stats">
<div><b>1</b><span>Crown mark at the root of every logo</span></div>
<div><b>5</b><span>Ministry sub-brands, one family</span></div>
<div><b>4</b><span>City identities across Poland</span></div>
<div><b>23</b><span>Pages of brand guidelines</span></div>
</div>
<p class="ct-art-lead">Kingdom Life was a vibrant, growing church across Poland whose brand felt like a placeholder. They already knew who they were. The problem was that their identity did not show it. The brief was simple: "Give us something that feels like us."</p>
<h2>The challenge</h2>
<p>Rebranding a church is unlike any corporate project. You are representing a mission, not selling a product, and the visual presence had become white noise. Three problems had to be solved at once.</p>
<ol class="ct-art-list">
<li><b>The pattern trap.</b> A Christian brand without the tired clichés of doves and globes.</li>
<li><b>A house of many rooms.</b> Men, Women, Worship and Missions each needed a personality, without breaking the family bond.</li>
<li><b>The scale of spirit.</b> From a tiny Instagram avatar to a 40-foot stage banner, without losing its weight.</li>
</ol>
<h2>The idea: a crown, built to flex</h2>
<p>We moved away from generic icons to a custom crown shape that represents divine authority, then built three versions so the mark holds at any size. The primary logo pairs crown and wordmark as the central identity. The icon works at small sizes, from profile pictures to app icons and patterns. The wordmark is the version people see most, on everyday touchpoints.</p>
<div class="ct-art-figs one"><figure class="ct-art-fig"><img alt="Kingdom Life primary logo: crown above the KINGDOM LIFE wordmark" loading="lazy" src="/img/kl-guide-logo.webp"/><figcaption>The primary logo, from the 23-page brand guidelines.</figcaption></figure></div>
<h2>The system</h2>
<h3>1. One family, five ministries</h3>
<p>Every ministry keeps the same crown and wordmark and gets its own colour, each with a reason behind it. Kingdom Women in magenta for joy, dignity and compassionate love. Kingdom Men in royal blue for strength and calm leadership. Worship in golden yellow for light and the glory of God's presence. Harvest Conference in purple for spiritual royalty. Missions in green for growth and new life. Each feels distinct, and all of them still read as Kingdom Life.</p>
<div class="ct-art-figs one"><figure class="ct-art-fig"><img alt="Ministry sub-brand rationale page from the guidelines" loading="lazy" src="/img/kl-guide-subbrands.webp"/><figcaption>Sub-brand rationale: one crown, a colour for each ministry.</figcaption></figure></div>
<h3>2. One crown, rooted in every city</h3>
<p>As the church expands across Poland, each city pairs the crown with its own local colours, so Kingdom Life feels familiar and part of the community. Warsaw takes the red of its coat of arms, Kraków the blue of its city mark, and Katowice and Częstochowa follow the same logic.</p>
<div class="ct-art-figs one"><figure class="ct-art-fig"><img alt="City identities for Kraków, Warszawa, Katowice and Częstochowa" loading="lazy" src="/img/kl-guide-cities.webp"/><figcaption>City identities: Kraków, Warszawa, Katowice and Częstochowa.</figcaption></figure></div>
<h3>3. Every colour means something</h3>
<p>Black, off-white and USC Gold carry the main brand, and the five secondary colours belong to the ministries. A strict type hierarchy in SF Pro, with Citadel as a secondary script, makes every sermon title and social post feel intentional rather than accidental.</p>
<div class="ct-art-figs two"><figure class="ct-art-fig"><img alt="Primary colours: black, off-white and USC gold" loading="lazy" src="/img/kl-guide-primary.webp"/><figcaption>Primary colours: Black #050505, Off-white #E2E2E2, USC Gold #FFCC00.</figcaption></figure><figure class="ct-art-fig"><img alt="Secondary colours for the ministries" loading="lazy" src="/img/kl-guide-secondary.webp"/><figcaption>Secondary colours, one per ministry.</figcaption></figure></div>
<blockquote class="ct-art-quote">From a brand that just exists to a brand that communicates with authority.</blockquote>
<h2>From pulpit to profile picture</h2>
<p>Social templates, photography and merchandise all draw on the same crown, colours and type. Bold headlines, the black, off-white and gold palette and the crown as a mark or pattern give every sermon post, invite and piece of merch the same voice.</p>
<div class="ct-art-figs three"><figure class="ct-art-fig"><img alt="Seek ye first the kingdom of God scripture post on gold" loading="lazy" src="/img/kl-scripture.webp"/><figcaption>Scripture post.</figcaption></figure><figure class="ct-art-fig"><img alt="For the King sermon series post" loading="lazy" src="/img/kl-sermon-series.webp"/><figcaption>Sermon series.</figcaption></figure><figure class="ct-art-fig"><img alt="The kingdom of God is at hand post with a crown of thorns" loading="lazy" src="/img/kl-verse.webp"/><figcaption>Verse of the week.</figcaption></figure></div>
<div class="ct-art-figs three"><figure class="ct-art-fig"><img alt="Never miss a sermon app launch post" loading="lazy" src="/img/kl-app.webp"/><figcaption>App launch.</figcaption></figure><figure class="ct-art-fig"><img alt="See you at church service invite" loading="lazy" src="/img/kl-invite.webp"/><figcaption>Service invite.</figcaption></figure><figure class="ct-art-fig"><img alt="Born again message graphic" loading="lazy" src="/img/kl-message.webp"/><figcaption>Message graphic.</figcaption></figure></div>
<div class="ct-art-figs three"><figure class="ct-art-fig"><img alt="Kingdom Life t-shirt worn by a woman on an orange chair" loading="lazy" src="/img/kl-tee.webp"/><figcaption>Merch tee.</figcaption></figure><figure class="ct-art-fig"><img alt="Black tote bag with the crown pattern" loading="lazy" src="/img/kl-tote.webp"/><figcaption>Tote with the crown pattern.</figcaption></figure><figure class="ct-art-fig"><img alt="Pastor portrait with the Kingdom Life mark" loading="lazy" src="/img/kl-pastor.webp"/><figcaption>Brand photography.</figcaption></figure></div>
<h2>The results</h2>
<p>Church growth goes beyond design, so the results here are what the system delivers: clear differentiation from generic church branding, unified recognition across five ministries and four cities, and consistency across digital and physical touchpoints. Kingdom Life now has one system its ministries and cities can grow into.</p>
<div class="ct-art-results">
<div><span>Logo versions</span><b>3, from avatar to stage</b><i style="--w:38%"></i></div>
<div><span>Ministry sub-brands</span><b>5, one family</b><i style="--w:62%"></i></div>
<div><span>Brand colours</span><b>8, each with a meaning</b><i style="--w:100%"></i></div>
</div>
<h2>What we took from it</h2>
<ul class="ct-art-list">
<li><b>Mission before motif.</b> A custom symbol with meaning beats borrowed clichés every time.</li>
<li><b>Give every room its own light.</b> Sub-brands can be distinct and still clearly part of one family.</li>
<li><b>Write it down.</b> A 23-page guide is what keeps a growing, volunteer-run brand consistent.</li>
</ul>
